; COBRA -- Elite's Cobra Mk III, tumbling on three axes, in CoolAction!
;
; **Every frame is computed between two vertical blanks** (D105): the
; rotation from three angles, the 28 vertices through it and into
; perspective, the 13 faces culled by their normals through it, the
; visible edges drawn -- sixty times a second. What is not
; computed is what a machine of this kind has always carried: sine, the
; reciprocal of depth, and the model, which tools/mk3d.py writes into the
; block below from the published Elite source.
;
; It is no longer the BASIC's twin. demos/cobra.bas still spins 72
; precomputed frames about one axis; this is what the compiled machine
; does when it is not copying one.
;
; **The screen is a mode of its own**: mode 6's full height, 256 x 240,
; at 4 bits a pixel -- 30,720 bytes a page, so two pages fit VRAM, at
; $0000 and $7800, and the display flips between them (D92). Mode 5 is
; the same depth and gives 48 of the 240 lines to borders; mode 6 at 8
; bits is 61,440 bytes a page and cannot double-buffer at all. The pixel
; port writes a 4-bit pixel in one store and steps X or Y itself, which
; is the fastest thing on the machine for a line.
;
; **The flip goes before the wait, not after it.** The fetch takes
; VID_DBASE at the frame start, and the frame counter WaitVBlank watches
; ticks on the same pulse (cool8_vregs.v), so a page flipped after
; WaitVBlank() reaches the glass a frame late -- and the page drawn on
; next is the one on show, erased and redrawn in full view. Flipped when
; it is finished, a page is taken at the next frame start, and only then
; is the other one drawn on.
;
; **Where the clocks are spent in assembly**: Transform (a multiply-add
; is 28 clocks and has no branch, because every signed value carries 128
; into the unsigned MUL and the surplus comes back off as two constants),
; Faces (the same multiply-add, over the normals), and Lines (a pixel is
; one store and an error step, 14 clocks, and 23 on a pixel where the
; other axis steps). The rest -- the rotation's fifteen products, the
; edge list -- is compiled, and is where the profile says it can stay.
;
; Runs until a key is pressed.

; ---- generated by tools/mk3d.py: do not edit ----
; the Cobra Mk III as bbcelite.com publishes it: 28 vertices, each as
; x+128 y+128 z+128 and the 16-bit 128*(x+y+z) Transform takes back off;
; 128 is 127 here, the one coordinate a signed byte cannot hold
BYTE ARRAY vb(140) = [
  160 128 204 0 54 96 128 204 0 22 128 154 152 0 25
  8 125 120 128 190 248 125 120 128 54 40 144 88 0 200
  216 144 88 0 32 255 120 88 128 39 1 120 88 128 168
  128 154 88 0 249 96 104 88 0 208 160 104 88 0 240
  92 136 88 0 222 120 140 88 0 238 136 140 88 0 246
  164 136 88 0 2 164 116 88 0 248 136 112 88 0 232
  120 112 88 0 224 92 116 88 0 212 128 128 204 0 38
  128 128 218 0 45 48 122 88 0 193 48 134 88 0 199
  40 128 88 0 192 208 134 88 0 23 216 128 88 0 24
  208 122 88 0 17]
; 38 edges: their two vertices, and the two faces they border
BYTE ARRAY ea(38) = [
  0 0 1 3 4 6 6 5 5 2 2 3 4 1 0 8 10 7 1
  0 1 0 20 12 18 14 16 15 14 13 12 2 22 23 22 25 26 25]
BYTE ARRAY eb(38) = [
  1 4 3 8 7 7 9 9 8 5 6 5 6 2 2 10 11 11 10
  11 5 6 21 13 19 15 17 16 17 18 19 9 24 24 23 26 27 27]
BYTE ARRAY ef1(38) = [
  0 4 3 7 8 8 6 5 7 1 2 3 4 0 0 9 9 9 10
  11 1 2 0 9 9 9 9 9 9 9 9 5 9 9 9 9 9 9]
BYTE ARRAY ef2(38) = [
  11 12 10 10 12 9 9 9 9 5 6 7 8 1 2 10 11 12 11
  12 3 4 11 9 9 9 9 9 9 9 9 6 9 9 9 9 9 9]
; 13 faces: the normal of each one's own triangle of the vertices above,
; 127 long, as nx+128 ny+128 nz+128 and the 16-bit 128*(nx+ny+nz) + T,
; T being where the rotated normal's z turns the face to the viewer
BYTE ARRAY nb(65) = [
  128 242 185 60 81 90 244 162 60 52 166 244 162 60 90
  92 245 162 218 53 164 245 162 218 89 114 254 128 201 52
  142 254 128 201 66 61 236 126 230 11 195 236 126 230 78
  128 128 1 128 187 107 5 153 237 193 128 4 154 17 205
  149 5 153 237 214]
; sine, 127 to a unit, 1024 steps to a turn: cosine is sn(a + 256)
INT ARRAY sn(1024) = [
  0 1 2 2 3 4 5 5 6 7 8 9 9 10 11 12
  12 13 14 15 16 16 17 18 19 19 20 21 22 22 23 24
  25 26 26 27 28 29 29 30 31 32 32 33 34 35 35 36
  37 38 38 39 40 41 41 42 43 44 44 45 46 46 47 48
